  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nContinued dry and seasonable today under the influence of high \npressure. Clouds will increase later today and tonight out ahead of \nthe approach of the remnants of Isaias. Conditions will remain dry \nthrough Friday night, with overnight lows not progged to be as \nchilly owing to increased flow and cloud cover. \n\nAfternoon relative humidity will be low once again, generally in the \n30 percent range across much of the area, but with winds expected to \ncontinue to be light, fire danger risk remains low. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nRemnants of Isaias are progged to move into the TN valley region by \nlate Saturday night into Sunday. Southeasterly flow on the periphery \nof Isaias will take hold across the area, with mtns and perhaps \nparts of SE Ohio seeing precipitation first, with parts of WV \nlowlands in favored downslope regions remaining drier at the start, \nbefore the center of the low nudges closer to the region as the day \nprogresses on Sunday, and flow veers into a more \nsoutherly/southwesterly direction with time.  \n\nThe low will move east across the area Sunday night into Monday, \nwith showery conditions lingering. High pressure will then build in \nfor early next week behind the departing low, with a brief break in \nthe rainy weather, before another frontal boundary affects the area \nmid week, providing for another chance of light precipitation. \n\nMountainous locations and SE Ohio could see significant rain from \nthis For now, those areas are in a marginal risk for excessive \nrainfall. But the period will need to be watched for the potential \nfor flooding of area creeks and streams, particularly in the eastern \ncounties and southeast Ohio. Dry conditions leading up to the event \nshould hopefully prevent much in the way of significant or overly \nwidespread flooding issues.   \n\nIn addition, the risk for tree fall will be increased across the \narea during the period, particularly as the ground becomes saturated \nin locations such as the higher terrain, as winds increase with the \napproach of Isaias.  This of course will lead to an increased risk \nof power outages during the period.",
